1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:01,952
A caller wants two mugs.

2
00:00:02,232 --> 00:00:03,439
There are five left.

3
00:00:03,719 --> 00:00:07,005
What happens if someone at the next desk sells them while
we're on the phone?

4
00:00:07,285 --> 00:00:08,806
Let's start Stockroom locally.

5
00:00:09,086 --> 00:00:10,467
Docker runs Postgres.

6
00:00:10,747 --> 00:00:13,940
The README lists the environment settings and
dependencies.

7
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:19,073
The seed adds six products, and the dev command starts the
console and its API.

8
00:00:20,003 --> 00:00:20,769
Here's the shelf.

9
00:00:21,049 --> 00:00:22,919
The Studio Mug has five left.

10
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:26,473
The Trail Bottle has zero, so its add button is disabled.

11
00:00:26,753 --> 00:00:29,400
I'll put two mugs and a Field Notebook in the draft.

12
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:31,967
That's forty-eight rupees and fifty paise.

13
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:35,544
The mugs are still available until I actually place the
order.

14
00:00:36,474 --> 00:00:37,600
Now I'll place it.

15
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:40,272
Here's the reference I can read back to the caller.

16
00:00:40,552 --> 00:00:44,580
The server priced each line in whole paise and saved the
prices with the order.

17
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:48,970
Both mugs and the notebook came out of stock in the same
database transaction.

18
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:54,525
If the caller also asks for the sold-out bottle, Stockroom
refuses the whole order.

19
00:00:54,845 --> 00:00:57,643
The mugs stay on the shelf, and no order is created.

20
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:05,383
At the packing desk, I open the dashboard and choose To
pack.

21
00:01:05,663 --> 00:01:09,715
If the caller rings back, I can type the last few
characters of their reference.

22
00:01:09,995 --> 00:01:13,130
Opening that result shows exactly what goes in the box.

23
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:14,049
Two mugs.

24
00:01:14,329 --> 00:01:15,281
One notebook.

25
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:16,954
Now I can mark it packed.

26
00:01:17,884 --> 00:01:19,741
Packed puts it in the ready to ship queue.

27
00:01:20,021 --> 00:01:22,796
I can leave it here until the courier actually takes it.

28
00:01:23,076 --> 00:01:24,945
After handoff, I mark it shipped.

29
00:01:25,225 --> 00:01:28,999
The timeline keeps the three timestamps, and this order is
complete.

30
00:01:29,929 --> 00:01:32,216
Here's a different caller who changes their mind.

31
00:01:32,496 --> 00:01:34,992
Their order is still placed, so I can cancel it.

32
00:01:35,272 --> 00:01:38,279
The confirmation tells me the units will go back on the
shelf.

33
00:01:38,559 --> 00:01:41,717
Once I confirm, the dashboard records the cancellation.

34
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:44,446
The same option is available on a packed order.

35
00:01:45,376 --> 00:01:47,571
What if someone sends a bad request directly?

36
00:01:47,851 --> 00:01:51,949
In a fresh test database I asked for two mugs and one
sold-out bottle.

37
00:01:52,229 --> 00:01:54,017
The server rejected the whole cart.

38
00:01:54,297 --> 00:01:57,002
Five mugs remained, and there were zero orders.

39
00:01:57,282 --> 00:01:58,954
Two mugs on their own went through.

40
00:01:59,234 --> 00:02:00,627
Stock dropped to three.

41
00:02:00,907 --> 00:02:02,718
Trying to jump straight to shipped failed.

42
00:02:02,998 --> 00:02:04,519
Cancelling restored five.

43
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:08,154
Cancelling again returned a conflict, and the count stayed
at five.

44
00:02:09,084 --> 00:02:12,324
The React interface keeps a draft in the tab with Zustand.

45
00:02:12,604 --> 00:02:15,750
TanStack Query refreshes the catalogue after checkout.

46
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:19,919
Nest and Fastify receive the request, and Zod checks its
shape.

47
00:02:20,199 --> 00:02:24,623
Drizzle locks the product rows in a consistent order
before checking availability.

48
00:02:24,903 --> 00:02:28,003
On cancellation, the server locks the order first.

49
00:02:28,283 --> 00:02:32,787
A second request sees that it's already cancelled before
it can add anything back.

50
00:02:33,717 --> 00:02:36,179
The README includes the commands to reproduce this.

51
00:02:36,459 --> 00:02:43,088
Nineteen API tests passed, including placement,
insufficient stock, and an invalid state change.

52
00:02:43,368 --> 00:02:45,307
Eleven browser tests passed as well.

53
00:02:45,587 --> 00:02:48,745
Then two server processes competed for the same stock.

54
00:02:49,025 --> 00:02:54,423
Out of a hundred and twenty checkout attempts, twelve
orders took the twenty-four available notebooks.

55
00:02:54,703 --> 00:02:56,468
The other requests got a conflict.

56
00:02:56,748 --> 00:03:01,821
A restart preserved all thirteen orders from the full run
and the exact inventory.

57
00:03:02,751 --> 00:03:06,571
Back at the desk, the shipped parcel and the cancelled
order are both searchable.

58
00:03:06,851 --> 00:03:11,693
The next person answering the phone can see what happened,
and how much stock they can promise.
